Setting: X is a smooth curve over S. Over C, with X compact, the analytic side is used and GAGA closes the argument. Grothendieck is proving the equivalence of several descriptions of a projective connection (projective structure) c_0 on X/S. (a) A P^1-bundle P → X with a flat connection and a section σ that is nowhere horizontal. (d) A second-order linear differential equation, Schwarzian-type. (e) A sheaf 𝒰 of germs of local analytic maps X^an → P^1(C), étale and pairwise PGL(2,C)-conjugate. 𝒰 is a left torsor under the constant sheaf PGL(2,C)_X, i.e. a projective atlas.

1) Top of page: end of the passage (a)→(e). The flat connection trivialises P^an over a small neighbourhood U of x, giving P^an|U ≅ U × P_x. Choosing an isomorphism P_x ≅ P^1(C), the section σ becomes a map U → P^1(C). These maps, as the trivialisation varies (it is unique up to PGL2), are the germs making up 𝒰.

2) Interpretation of local horizontal sections of P in terms of (e). P is associated to the principal PGL2-bundle 𝒞 = Isom(P^1_X, P), with induced flat connection. Horizontal sections of P are obtained by applying horizontal sections of 𝒞 to fixed points of P^1(C). Concretely, the horizontal section through p ∈ P_{x0} comes from:
- choosing f_{x0} ∈ 𝒞_{x0};
- setting p_0 = f_{x0}^{-1}(p);
- taking x ↦ f̃_{x0}(x)(p_0), where f̃ is the horizontal extension of f_{x0}.
Equivalently, P = 𝒞/B with B a Borel subgroup of PGL2, i.e. the stabiliser of a point (σ gives the B-reduction). So local horizontal sections of P are horizontal sections of 𝒞 modulo B, i.e. sections of 𝒰/B^an. Here 𝒰 ⊂ Hom_hol(X^an, P^1), and B is the affine group fixing ∞. Thus a horizontal section is a compatible local affine structure: projective charts up to affine transformations.

3) A section of P disjoint from σ is identified with a connection D: ω → ω^{⊗2} on ω = Ω^1_{X/S}. In the following page this uses principal parts P^1_{X/S}(ω). Under this identification, horizontality means that the projective connection c_D induced by D equals c_0. So horizontal sections disjoint from σ correspond to affine connections subordinate to c_0.

4) The last line announces passing to 𝒞/B_u, with B_u the unipotent radical, i.e. a G_m-torsor over the complement of σ. The next page develops this:
- sections are pairs (D, φ), with φ a trivialisation (of ω^2, or more generally L^2 ≅ ω^3 with L a 'square root of ω^3');
- horizontality there is Dφ = 0;
- an explicit Riccati/Schwarzian-type computation follows: D_s = D_t + s' d(1/s'), giving the equation −ds'/s' = ϖ.

Purpose: an algebraic, relative dictionary between projective connections, PGL2-bundles with flat connection and transverse B-reduction (opers), projective atlases, and affine connections. This likely serves the study of Gauss–Manin/Picard–Fuchs equations and the Cartier operator, as the file title suggests (p-curvature of the uniformising equation).
